+# Maintaining this Cloudflare OS fork
+
+This repository is a downstream fork of Cloudflare OS with a deployment-specific AI Gateway custom-model feature.
+
+## Remote and branch layout
+
+- `upstream` — `https://github.com/cloudflare/cloudflare-os.git`; Cloudflare's source. Never push here.
+- `origin` — `https://github.com/dscott98/cloudflare-os.git`; this fork.
+- `custom/main` — the maintained downstream integration branch. It contains the custom feature on top of upstream.
+- `release-*` tags — immutable deployed revisions.
+
+The initial downstream feature commit is:
+
+```text
+3835c6d feat: add deployment AI Gateway custom models
+```
+
+## Normal development
+
+Never work directly on the local `main` branch. Start a feature branch from `custom/main`:
+
+```bash
+git switch custom/main
+git pull --ff-only origin custom/main
+git switch -c feature/<short-description>
+```
+
+When the work is reviewed and tested, commit it and push the feature branch:
+
+```bash
+git add <intended-files>
+git diff --cached
+git commit -m "<meaningful description>"
+git push -u origin HEAD
+```
+
+Merge it into `custom/main` through a pull request on the fork. Do not push directly to `main`.
+
+## Before committing
+
+Never use `git add -A` in this repository. Add intended files explicitly, then inspect the staged diff:
+
+```bash
+git diff --cached --check
+git diff --cached
+```
+
+Local environment files such as `.env.CF` are ignored. Do not force-add `.env*`, `.dev.vars*`, or `wrangler.dev.jsonc` files.
+
+## Updating from Cloudflare upstream
+
+Run this whenever you choose to adopt the latest upstream `main`:
+
+```bash
+# 1. Start from a clean downstream branch.
+git switch custom/main
+git status --short
+
+# 2. Download upstream changes. This does not modify working files.
+git fetch upstream --tags
+
+# 3. Preserve a local reference to the pre-update branch.
+git branch backup/custom-main-before-update
+
+# 4. Replay downstream commits onto the new upstream main.
+git rebase upstream/main
+```
+
+### If Git reports a conflict
+
+1. See which files conflict:
+
+   ```bash
+   git status
+   ```
+
+2. Open each conflicted file. Resolve the sections marked:
+
+   ```text
+   <<<<<<<
+   =======
+   >>>>>>>
+   ```
+
+3. Preserve both the new upstream behavior and the downstream custom-model feature. Pay particular attention to:
+
+   ```text
+   packages/workshop-shared/src/api.ts
+   packages/workshop-backend/src/admin-config.ts
+   packages/workshop-backend/src/admin-settings.ts
+   packages/workshop-backend/src/user.ts
+   packages/workshop-backend/src/ai-models.ts
+   packages/workshop-backend/src/server.ts
+   ```
+
+4. Mark each resolved file and continue:
+
+   ```bash
+   git add <resolved-file>
+   git rebase --continue
+   ```
+
+5. If the rebase becomes confusing, safely return to the state before it began:
+
+   ```bash
+   git rebase --abort
+   ```
+
+## Verify an upstream update
+
+After a successful rebase:
+
+```bash
+pnpm lint && pnpm build && pnpm test
+
+git range-diff \
+  upstream/main...backup/custom-main-before-update \
+  upstream/main...custom/main
+```
+
+`range-diff` shows whether rebasing unexpectedly changed the downstream patch.
+
+If verification succeeds, update the fork. Rebasing creates new commit IDs, so this command is expected:
+
+```bash
+git push --force-with-lease origin custom/main
+```
+
+`--force-with-lease` refuses to overwrite an unexpected remote update. Never force-push a release tag or release branch.
+
+## Creating a deployable release
+
+After testing the exact revision to deploy:
+
+```bash
+git switch custom/main
+git tag release-YYYY-MM-DD
+git push origin release-YYYY-MM-DD
+```
+
+Deploy that tag/commit, not an unrecorded moving branch head.
