+# Gadgets implementation review
+
+## What a Gadget is
+
+A **Gadget** is a private, full-stack personal app—not a public SaaS endpoint.
+
+It has:
+
+- `server.js`: a Cloudflare Durable Object class exported as `Gadget`
+- `client.js`: browser UI JavaScript, rendered in a sandboxed iframe
+- optional supporting `.js` modules and `README.md`
+- private Durable Object storage
+- explicitly granted external-resource bindings
+
+Important terminology: `openGadget()` and `GadgetMetadata` are historical names for a **workspace**. A workspace now contains zero or more Gadget workpieces, plus chats, Gatekeeper connections, sharing, and code history.
+
+## Runtime architecture
+
+```text
+Browser Workshop UI
+  └─ persistent Cap’n Web WebSocket RPC
+       └─ Workshop backend
+            └─ one Overseer Durable Object per workspace
+                 ├─ Yjs source-code history, chats, sharing, bindings
+                 ├─ gadget registry
+                 ├─ Gatekeeper facets / approval queue
+                 └─ one Dynamic Worker Durable Object facet per Gadget
+                      ├─ server.js
+                      ├─ private DO storage
+                      └─ explicitly injected bindings only
+
+Sandboxed iframe
+  └─ client.js ↔ MessagePort Cap’n Web RPC ↔ Gadget server facet
+```
+
+Core implementation locations:
+
+- Shared RPC contract: `packages/workshop-shared/src/api.ts`
+- Workspace kernel and gadget runtime: `packages/workshop-backend/src/overseer.ts`
+- Agent tools and Gadget authoring instructions: `packages/workshop-backend/src/agent.ts`
+- Public/authenticated workspace creation: `packages/workshop-backend/src/server.ts`
+- Iframe sandbox and RPC handshake: `packages/workshop-frontend/src/GadgetUI.tsx`
+- Full editor and multi-Gadget picker: `packages/workshop-frontend/src/GadgetEditor.tsx`
+
+## How source and state work
+
+A Gadget’s **source code** is stored in the workspace’s Yjs document.
+
+- Each Gadget has its own named Yjs root containing files.
+- Code updates are versioned, replayable, collaboratively editable, and may be proposed in a chat before acceptance.
+- Each Gadget’s **runtime state** belongs in its Durable Object storage, not memory.
+- Each Gadget gets a Dynamic Worker facet named independently from other Gadgets, so changing/testing Gadget A restarts A—not Gadget B.
+
+A newly agent-created Gadget is initially **provisional** to that chat. Its code, creation, and new bindings become permanent only when the user accepts the chat’s proposed changes. Reverting removes it.
+
+## How to build one
+
+### Use the built-in agent
+
+A typical request is simply:
+
+> “Make me a project tracker.”
+
+The agent should:
+
+1. Check available Blueprints.
+2. Call `createGadget(title, bindingName, blueprintId?)`.
+3. Write `server.js` and `client.js`.
+4. Test it through the preview.
+5. Present proposed changes for acceptance.
+
+A minimal manually authored Gadget is:
+
+```js
+// server.js
+import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";
+
+export class Gadget extends DurableObject {
+  greet(name) {
+    return `Hello, ${name}!`;
+  }
+}
+```
+
+```js
+// client.js
+const text = await gadget.greet("World");
+document.body.append(text);
+```
+
+There is no `index.html`, no public `fetch()` handler, and no normal deployment step per Gadget.
+
+### Build manually in the editor
+
+Use the workspace’s **Code** tab to edit files. The frontend syncs Yjs updates to the Overseer; the runtime loader rebuilds the relevant Gadget facet when needed.
+
+For persistent state, use normal Durable Object storage APIs in `server.js`. For real-time collaboration, clients can pass Cap’n Web callbacks to server subscription methods.
+
+## How integrations work
+
+A Gadget starts with access to nothing.
+
+External services are represented by **Gatekeepers**, not raw API keys or unrestricted network access.
+
+```text
+User connects a resource
+  → Gatekeeper capability is created in the workspace
+  → agent may use it temporarily in chat env.NAME
+  → agent calls setGadgetBinding() only if Gadget code needs it persistently
+  → Gadget server sees it as this.env.NAME
+```
+
+This distinction matters:
+
+- The agent’s `executeCode` environment is **chat-scoped**.
+- A Gadget’s `this.env` is **Gadget-scoped**.
+- Giving the agent access to GitHub does not automatically give Gadget code GitHub access.
+
+Gatekeepers enforce narrow resource scope, OAuth/account handling, observation logging, and approval queues for side effects. Gadgets can call only capabilities explicitly bound into their environment.
+
+## Security model
+
+The implementation has strong, intentional boundaries:
+
+- Dynamic Gadget workers load with `globalOutbound: null`; Gadget code cannot use the public internet.
+- The browser UI is an iframe with a restrictive CSP and no network access.
+- Browser-to-server communication is capability RPC over a MessagePort.
+- Binding names are centrally validated against JavaScript reserved words and prototype-pollution names.
+- Shared “use” collaborators receive a restricted capability that can render/interact with the UI but cannot access source, chat, bindings, or management APIs.
+- A collaborator’s third-party accounts are not implicitly shared; added bindings use the adding collaborator’s account.
+- Sensitive observations can block sharing or require Gatekeeper observer verification.
+
+Relevant files:
+
+- Dynamic worker isolation: `packages/workshop-backend/src/overseer.ts`
+- Iframe CSP: `packages/workshop-frontend/src/GadgetUI.tsx`
+- Sharing model: `docs/sharing.md`
+- Observer/information-flow design: `docs/observers.md`
+
+## Blueprints and formats
+
+A **Blueprint** is a reusable Gadget source snapshot.
+
+It includes:
+
+- source files;
+- binding requirements and guidance;
+- metadata, author, version, and optional screenshot.
+
+It does **not** include:
+
+- runtime database/storage;
+- credentials or live Gatekeeper connections;
+- chat/edit history.
+
+Users can publish, share by link, export/import `.gadget` archives, or instantiate a Blueprint after configuring their own bindings.
+
+A **format** is an admin-curated Blueprint presented as “New Doc”, “New Slides”, etc. Bundled formats live in:
+
+```text
+packages/workshop-backend/format-blueprints/
+```
+
+To update one, build it in Workshop, export it, then import it:
+
+```bash
+pnpm import:format-blueprint ~/Downloads/MyExport.gadget format.document
+```
+
+For a fork, prefer `FORMAT_BLUEPRINTS_DIR` to maintain a separate format directory rather than modifying the upstream set. See:
+
+```text
+packages/workshop-backend/format-blueprints/README.md
+docs/blueprints.md
+```
+
+## Review findings
+
+### [P1] Core Gadget lifecycle lacks active end-to-end coverage
+
+There are no active backend tests exercising `createGadget`, Dynamic Worker loading, provisional Gadget acceptance/revert, or Blueprint-to-multi-Gadget instantiation. The existing RPC integration suite at `packages/workshop-backend/__integration__/open-gadget-rpc.test.ts` is currently `describe.skip(...)`.
+
+This is the most important gap because multi-Gadget state crosses Yjs, Durable Object storage, Dynamic Worker facets, RPC capabilities, and chat replay. Add integration coverage before making major changes in this area.
+
+### [P2] The kernel seam is very large
+
+The main implementation is concentrated in:
+
+```text
+packages/workshop-backend/src/overseer.ts  # ~9.5k lines
+packages/workshop-backend/src/agent.ts     # ~3.2k lines
+```
+
+The architecture is coherent, but these will be recurring upstream-rebase conflict zones. Keep custom Gadget behavior in narrow helpers/tests rather than editing loader, replay, or capability code casually.
+
+### [P2] Workspace sharing is not per-Gadget sharing
+
+A workspace may contain many Gadgets, but sharing remains workspace-wide. A collaborator granted access can access all non-pending Gadgets in that workspace according to their role. If users need to share only one app, create it in its own workspace or share a Blueprint instead.
+
+### [P2] Persistent callbacks have a multi-Gadget limitation
+
+`ctx.restore()` calls originating from agent `executeCode` currently restore against the default/first Gadget. Avoid designing multi-Gadget automation where persistent callbacks must belong to a specific non-default Gadget until that TODO is resolved.
+
+## Security sweep
+
+- No tracked `.env`, certificate, key, or database files found.
+- No permissive CORS or disabled TLS-verification patterns found.
+- Credential-like matches are primarily expected OAuth/configuration/provider code and documentation; this was a quick sweep, not a credential audit.
